    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.chromatic_number_upper_bound - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.chromatic_number_upper_bound.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.chromatic_number_upper_bound()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Get an upper bound on the chromatic number from a coloring.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.chromatic_number_upper_bound()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Get an upper bound on the chromatic number from a coloring.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.chromatic_number_upper_bound()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n  .doc-label .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Get an upper bound on the chromatic number from a coloring.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nGet an upper bound on the chromatic number from a coloring.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L82692-L82791){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.chromatic_number_upper_bound(coloring=None)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Chromatic number upper bound](../assets/graphs/chromatic-number-upper-bound.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A five-node graph colored greedily so that no edge joins two nodes of the same color: C and E get color 0 (accent), A and D color 1 (teal), B color 2 (violet). The card counts three colors used, the upper bound 3.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nThe chromatic number is the minimum number of colors needed to color a graph so that no\ntwo adjacent nodes share a color. Computing it exactly is NP-hard, but any valid coloring\ngives an upper bound: if a coloring uses `k` colors, the chromatic number is at most `k`.\nThis method returns the number of distinct colors in a coloring.\n\nThis is useful for:\n\n- estimating how many time slots, frequencies, or resources a conflict graph needs\n- comparing coloring strategies (fewer colors means a tighter bound)\n- quickly checking whether a graph might be 2- or 3-colorable\n\nThe bound is only as good as the coloring. With no argument, the method runs\n`greedy_color()` with its default `\"largest_first\"` strategy; passing a coloring from a\nstronger strategy such as `\"dsatur\"` can give a tighter bound. When a coloring is\nsupplied, it is *not* checked for validity, so pass it through `is_valid_coloring()`\nfirst if it did not come from `greedy_color()`; an invalid coloring does not give a\ntrue upper bound.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[coloring]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [dict[int, int] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   A dictionary mapping node IDs to integer colors, typically the output of\n    `greedy_color()`. Only the number of distinct values is used. If `None` (the\n    default), a greedy coloring is computed first using the `\"largest_first\"` strategy.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The number of distinct colors used in the coloring (`0` for an empty coloring or an\n    empty graph).\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~greedy_color`) \n\n:   The coloring the bound comes from.\n\n[](`~clique_number`) \n\n:   A lower bound on the number of colors needed.\n\n[](`~is_valid_coloring`) \n\n:   Check a coloring before taking its bound.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA triangle needs three colors, since every node is next to the other two. A square needs\nonly two, as it alternates around:\n\n::: {#1deee36d .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ntriangle = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(3)\nsquare = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(4)\n\n(\n    triangle.chromatic_number_upper_bound(),\n    square.chromatic_number_upper_bound(),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(3, 2)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### How many exam slots a college needs\n\n`exams()` joins two of a made-up college's courses when some student takes both, so\ntheir exams can't share a time slot. With no coloring passed, the bound comes from the\ndefault greedy coloring, which needs five slots. DSATUR finds a timetable with four:\n\n::: {#1e14f4a2 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nexams = ec.exams()\ndsatur = exams.greedy_color(strategy=\"dsatur\")\n\n(\n    exams.chromatic_number_upper_bound(),\n    exams.chromatic_number_upper_bound(dsatur),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(5, 4)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nAn upper bound can only say that four slots are enough. Calculus, Physics, Chemistry and\nBiology all share students with one another, so they need four different slots, and four\nis the fewest:\n\n::: {#cd647c13 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nclique = exams.maximum_clique()\nlabel = exams.get_label_from_id\n\nexams.clique_number(), sorted(label(n) for n in clique)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(4, ['Biology', 'Calculus', 'Chemistry', 'Physics'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#29cbedbf .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nexams.set_node_attrs(\"slot\", dsatur).plot(\n    color_by=\"slot\", highlight=clique, label_by=\"label\", seed=3,\n    height=420,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fi